     1	# DaoStream Design
     2	
     3	Status: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does
     4	not redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only
     5	after this contract is implemented and tested.
     6	
     7	## Purpose
     8	
     9	This contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable
    10	invariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it
    11	directly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate
    12	them:
    13	
    14	1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only
    15	   streams.
    16	2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge
    17	   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.
    18	
    19	DaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because
    20	interpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is
    21	what makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries
    22	values and decides nothing about them:
    23	
    24	- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element
    25	  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody
    26	  has taken yet.
    27	- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who
    28	  is listening would be hidden global state.
    29	- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — "do not introduce
    30	  callbacks; every callback is events on a stream", seen from the stream's
    31	  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and
    32	  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).
    33	
    34	The Invariants below are these denials in operational form.
    35	
    36	The design separates four concepts:
    37	
    38	1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.
    39	2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the
    40	   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.
    41	3. A **portable descriptor** is data used to attach to an existing stream.
    42	4. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one
    43	   logical stream.
    44	
    45	Creation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification. A
    46	handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned state.
    47	
    48	Seven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see
    49	Result Convention):
    50	
    51	| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |
    52	|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|
    53	| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |
    54	| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |
    55	| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |
    56	| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |
    57	| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |
    58	| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |
    59	| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |
    60	
    61	A handle implements the subset of these operations its transport
    62	declares (see Surfaces).
    63	
    64	## Invariants
    65	
    66	- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.
    67	- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.
    68	- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.
    69	- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.
    70	- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.
    71	- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase
    72	  retained history.
    73	- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap
    74	  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.
    75	- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.
    76	- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is
    77	  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.
    78	- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or
    79	  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.
    80	
    81	Every clause below derives from these invariants. A proposed addition that
    82	cannot be derived from one of them does not belong in this contract.
    83	
    84	## Result Convention
    85	
    86	Every DaoStream operation returns data.
    87	
    88	- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce
    89	  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of
    90	  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose
    91	  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every
    92	  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and
    93	  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.
    94	- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated
    95	  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in
    96	  addition to it.
    97	- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per
    98	  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is
    99	  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be
   100	  added without breaking existing consumers.
   101	- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of
   102	  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an
   103	  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,
   104	  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions
   105	  and never callbacks.
   106	- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.
   107	
   108	## The IO Model
   109	
   110	Three models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the
   111	third.
   112	
   113	**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,
   114	then returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as
   115	ordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something
   116	must wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it
   117	requires a host that can park at all.
   118	
   119	**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and
   120	returns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer
   121	arrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this
   122	is the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer
   123	decides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host
   124	without blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because
   125	awaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the
   126	caller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback
   127	is reached from a callback.
   128	
   129	**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is
   130	true now, including the answer "nothing yet, ask again." Nothing parks and
   131	nothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.
   132	This is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.
   133	
   134	`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream
   135	has a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this
   136	position holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same
   137	answer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a
   138	callback; the Result Convention's "never exceptions and never callbacks" is
   139	this model stated as a rule about results.
   140	
   141	The rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that
   142	cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is
   143	called, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as
   144	data, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A
   145	DaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be
   146	reported back. It is a question about now.
   147	
   148	### Why this model and not the other two
   149	
   150	The hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and
   151	asynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there
   152	is no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single
   153	event loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be
   154	occupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.
   155	
   156	A blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A
   157	callback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all
   158	of them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is
   159	the only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same
   160	operations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what
   161	lets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API
   162	differing by host.
   163	
   164	### What it costs
   165	
   166	Polling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader
   167	holding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which
   168	have data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather
   169	than an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The
   170	cost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so
   171	cadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness
   172	mechanism belongs there too.
   173	
   174	### The readiness extension
   175	
   176	This contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives
   177	`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the
   178	concern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.
   179	
   180	A future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on
   181	transports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for
   182	example a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already
   183	permits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of
   184	this contract may depend on it existing.
   185	
   186	## Creation and Attachment
   187	
   188	Creation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an
   189	existing one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.
   190	
   191	- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a
   192	  new logical stream. The handle's portable identity is available as a
   193	  descriptor via `descriptor`, whose outcome set is `{ok}` with
   194	  `:dao.stream/descriptor` — identity is universal and outlives any
   195	  attachment, so `descriptor` has no failure outcome.
   196	- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an
   197	  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching
   198	  never creates.
   199	- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only
   200	  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a
   201	  request to load a plugin.
   202	
   203	Streams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization
   204	boundary. An in-memory stream holds references, so a handle can ride it
   205	intact, and an interpreter that receives a handle simply uses it —
   206	every operation takes a handle, and attaching to what you already hold
   207	is meaningless. But a handle does not survive encoding, and any
   208	transport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —
   209	is a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those
   210	boundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a
   211	transport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a
   212	value it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)
   213	`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and
   214	resolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the
   215	streams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the
   216	files a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no
   217	registry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving
   218	somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other
   219	host, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —
   220	honestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   221	
   222	### Envelopes
   223	
   224	A **creation specification** is a map with one required key:
   225	
   226	- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the
   227	  dispatch key.
   228	
   229	Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace
   230	(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler
   231	— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its
   232	own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.
   233	
   234	The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object
   235	reference. Its exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are
   236	settled, and the key set that realizes them is TBD:
   237	
   238	- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch
   239	  key.
   240	- It names exactly one logical stream, and that identity is stable: the same
   241	  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.
   242	- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is
   243	  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is
   244	  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns
   245	  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.
   246	- Like an IOR, it may carry authorization: an optional `:dao.stream/shibi`
   247	  key holding Shibi capability tokens that gate whether an interpreter may
   248	  attach. Shibi is not yet designed or built; until it is, the key is
   249	  reserved, no attachment is gated, and the `:dao.stream/denied` outcome
   250	  reserved in the `attach!` table below is unreachable.
   251	
   252	For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`
   253	on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,
   254	a value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end,
   255	where `attach!` on the reconstructed value attaches to the stream it names —
   256	provided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and
   257	Attachment on reachability), and allowing that a transport which cannot know
   258	that at call time reports it later as data.
   259	
   260	Both envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive
   261	the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is
   262	structural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or
   263	identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —
   264	a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.
   265	
   266	`create!` outcomes:
   267	
   268	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |
   269	|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   270	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists.                  | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   271	| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |
   272	| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |
   273	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |
   274	
   275	`attach!` outcomes:
   276	
   277	| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |
   278	|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   279	| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | A handle on an attachment to the logical stream the descriptor names. Where establishment has an answer only the far end can give, that answer arrives later as data, on the channel the transport names (see Surfaces). | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   280	| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |
   281	| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |
   282	| `:dao.stream/denied`             | Attachment refused by capability gating (reserved; unreachable until Shibi exists). | —                    |
   283	| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |
   284	
   285	### Host Dispatch
   286	
   287	When a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —
   288	for example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on
   289	`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's
   290	`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:
   291	
   292	```clojure
   293	{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!
   294	                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}
   295	 :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}
   296	```
   297	
   298	The entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates
   299	no streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns
   300	no stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates
   301	one. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and
   302	returns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and
   303	the correct one: nothing here creates that.
   304	
   305	Entry values are **host-composed**: ordinarily closures produced by a
   306	transport constructor that already received whatever composition state the
   307	transport needs — a boundary's deposit destination and its admission
   308	declaration, a directory some composition keeps. The bare vars above are
   309	schematic. A bare namespace var could reach that state only through namespace
   310	globals, which is the hidden state this contract retires.
   311	
   312	This table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an
   313	ordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor
   314	standardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns
   315	`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.
   316	
   317	Code that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions
   318	directly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global
   319	registry and no namespace-load registration side effect.
   320	
   321	## Surfaces
   322	
   323	A handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can
   324	honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**
   325	(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and
   326	Retention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.
   327	
   328	`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it, because identity
   329	is universal and outlives any attachment. An operation a handle does not
   330	declare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there
   331	is no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when
   332	asked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may
   333	answer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the
   334	operation exists. A transport may also offer operations of its own beside the
   335	public surface; they are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream operations,
   336	and no consumer of this contract may depend on them.
   337	
   338	The reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as
   339	one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and
   340	re-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that
   341	promise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the
   342	host's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary
   343	— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a
   344	transport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the
   345	transport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per
   346	its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly
   347	defect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission
   348	rule in detail.)
   349	
   350	A transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it
   351	excludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is
   352	impossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never
   353	reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer
   354	evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never
   355	as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport
   356	that cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An
   357	exclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.
   358	
   359	### Where the asynchrony goes
   360	
   361	The IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous
   362	whether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in
   363	a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does
   364	not pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's
   365	boundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what
   366	arrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and
   367	every reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,
   368	when it chooses.
   369	
   370	This is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but
   371	that the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a
   372	retained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary
   373	code to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing
   374	stated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,
   375	and its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.
   376	
   377	## Cursors
   378	
   379	A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.
   380	
   381	- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**
   382	  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract
   383	  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained
   384	  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended
   385	  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport
   386	  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional
   387	  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is
   388	  malformed or not honored by this transport yields
   389	  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,
   390	  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream
   391	  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains
   392	  readable. `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with
   393	  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`, and
   394	  `:dao.stream/closed`.
   395	- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as
   396	  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never
   397	  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A
   398	  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.
   399	- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the
   400	  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter
   401	  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives
   402	  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the
   403	  descriptor envelope.
   404	- A cursor carries enough identity to detect misuse — the mismatch outcomes
   405	  in the Reading table depend on it.
   406	- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never
   407	  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a
   408	  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.
   409	- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and
   410	  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a
   411	  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.
   412	
   413	## Reading
   414	
   415	`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.
   416	It is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data
   417	answer.
   418	
   419	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |
   420	|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   421	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |
   422	| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |
   423	| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |
   424	| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |
   425	| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |
   426	| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |
   427	
   428	Reading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same
   429	position observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the
   430	other. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no
   431	take. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one
   432	reader's progress every other reader's data loss.
   433	
   434	## Writing
   435	
   436	`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:
   437	
   438	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Required keys |
   439	|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
   440	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | The value is accepted at the next position in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |
   441	| `:dao.stream/full`            | The transport does not accept the value; nothing was appended. Whether this is transient or permanent is the transport's declared nature (see Retention and Gaps). | —             |
   442	| `:dao.stream/invalid-value`   | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                                                     | —             |
   443	| `:dao.stream/closed`          | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                                         | —             |
   444	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The transport failed to perform the append; nothing was appended.                                                                                    | —             |
   445	
   446	Acceptance is handle-relative, as close outcomes are. **Which sequence** a
   447	handle's writer surface is on is part of the transport's declared nature: for a
   448	ring buffer it is the logical stream itself, so `ok` places the value in it; for
   449	a transport that carries values toward a stream elsewhere it is the ordered
   450	outbound path, and what becomes of the value at the far end is reported there,
   451	not here. No remote transport can say more — under *no operation waits*, an
   452	answer from the far end cannot arrive before `append!` returns.
   453	
   454	The writer decides what to do about any non-`ok` outcome; DaoStream does not
   455	retry, buffer, or notify on its behalf.
   456	
   457	## Close
   458	
   459	`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome set is `{ok}` — no
   460	other outcome exists for it. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope
   461	is also `:dao.stream/ok`.
   462	
   463	`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle that owns a stream — its
   464	creator, or its server-side host — closes the logical stream. An attached
   465	handle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the
   466	stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin
   467	later. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared
   468	nature.
   469	
   470	`:dao.stream/closed` and `:dao.stream/end` are handle-relative: they speak of
   471	what *this handle* is on. (Not every outcome is: `gap` is retention-relative
   472	and `cursor-mismatch` is logical-stream-relative.) After an attachment
   473	closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers
   474	`ok` — identity outlives any attachment; a reader surface, where the
   475	handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this
   476	handle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are
   477	minted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,
   478	not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.
   479	
   480	Closing the logical stream changes future availability only:
   481	
   482	- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.
   483	- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead
   484	  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.
   485	- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a
   486	  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.
   487	
   488	There is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are
   489	authoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.
   490	
   491	## Concurrency
   492	
   493	Each logical stream is one append-only sequence, and a handle's writer
   494	surface is on exactly one sequence — for some transports the logical stream
   495	itself, for others the ordered outbound path toward a stream elsewhere (see
   496	Writing). A successfully appended value occupies exactly one definite position
   497	in the sequence this handle's writer surface is on, and every operation's
   498	outcome reflects that sequence at one moment. DaoStream guarantees nothing
   499	further about concurrent operations.
   500	
   501	This follows from Axiom 2. The sequence a handle's surfaces are on is the one
   502	truth for that handle; each outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A
   503	reader that observes
   504	`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a
   505	contradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global
   506	view exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is
   507	answered by the sequence the handle's writer surface is on — the value has a
   508	position in it or it does not — and where that sequence is not observable from
   509	this handle, the answer is read where it is.
   510	What an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —
   511	a merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,
   512	and interpretation happens above the stream.
   513	
   514	## Retention and Gaps
   515	
   516	Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned and form part of the
   517	stream or writer surface's declared nature; a locally created stream records
   518	them through its creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts
   519	the oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send
   520	buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —
   521	`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or
   522	permanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the
   523	outcomes and standardizes no policy.
   524	
   525	**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared
   526	policy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader
   527	positions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred
   528	eviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's
   529	absence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed
   530	reader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it
   531	deliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is
   532	told what it missed.
   533	
   534	When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the
   535	gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides
   536	nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live
   537	telemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently
   538	skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it
   539	missed values.
   540	
   541	## Composition
   542	
   543	DaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything
   544	that transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its
   545	own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely
   546	outside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the
   547	pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.
   548	
   549	## Explicitly Absent
   550	
   551	Absent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:
   552	
   553	- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration
   554	  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.
   555	- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it
   556	  returns; operation results are authoritative.
   557	- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one
   558	  reader's progress every other reader's data loss.
   559	- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and
   560	  kept cursors cover repositioning.
   561	- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.
   562	- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.
   563	- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.

