     1	# DaoStream Implementation Plan — the WebSocket Slice
     2	
     3	Status: migration plan, derived from and subordinate to `dao.stream.md`
     4	(the contract, review-approved) and `dao.stream.ws.md` (the WebSocket
     5	specification). Where this plan and the contract disagree, the contract
     6	wins. This document is transient: it is consumed as the phases complete,
     7	while the contract remains. Revised against the `gpt-5.6-sol` plan review
     8	of 2026-08-30 (`collab/review-dao-stream-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log`).
     9	
    10	## Strategy
    11	
    12	Two deliberate narrowings:
    13	
    14	1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy
    15	   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a
    16	   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new
    17	   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,
    18	   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while
    19	   legacy keeps the system running.
    20	   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The
    21	   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not
    22	   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.
    23	2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical
    24	   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the
    25	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    26	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    27	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    28	   this slice stands.
    29	
    30	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    31	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    32	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    33	one happens to have.
    34	
    35	## Settled before Phase 1
    36	
    37	Three questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery
    38	pressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.
    39	
    40	**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes
    41	every operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes
    42	it — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a
    43	future, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on
    44	any host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable (`ok`,
    45	`invalid-descriptor`) and how the connection resolved arrives as deposited
    46	lifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see
    47	`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise
    48	leaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.
    49	
    50	The rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:
    51	the ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and
    52	the ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting
    53	an endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation
    54	needs host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,
    55	opening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`
    56	does: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports
    57	acquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the
    58	acquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.
    59	
    60	**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's
    61	public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must
    62	not gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what
    63	`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit
    64	destination *and its declared retention nature as data*, derived from the
    65	same creation specification that produced the handle, and refuses a
    66	reject-mode declaration at assembly. This is configuration provenance, not
    67	runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every non-`ok`
    68	deposit result tears the connection down.
    69	
    70	**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in
    71	Phase 2 and stable descriptor identity in Phase 3 are the same identity.
    72	It is allocated when a logical stream is created, and both cursors and
    73	descriptors denote it. Their *representations* need not match and need not
    74	be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per
    75	the contract — but they must refer to the same thing.
    76	
    77	## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)
    78	
    79	The protocol and data surface, with no transport. The seven operations are
    80	two kinds, and the split is the point:
    81	
    82	- **Five handle operations**, as protocols a handle implements per its
    83	  declared surface: `descriptor` (universal identity), `cursor` and `next`
    84	  (reader), `append!` (writer), `close!` (closable).
    85	- **Two transport entry functions**, `create!` and `attach!`, which take no
    86	  handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions.
    87	  Dynamic selection is a host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to a
    88	  `{create attach}` pair — ordinary data, ordinary lookup, performed by the
    89	  host. DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery: no multimethod, no registry,
    90	  no load-time side effect. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example
    91	  map in tests.
    92	
    93	Putting `create!` or `attach!` on a protocol is how the retired registry
    94	grows back; the file must not do it.
    95	
    96	Also in Phase 1:
    97	
    98	- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by
    99	  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under
   100	  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the
   101	  contract's tables state (including the reserved `:dao.stream/denied` and
   102	  `:dao.stream/invalid-value`).
   103	- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;
   104	  everything else transport-owned.
   105	- `descriptor` on every handle regardless of surface — identity is
   106	  universal and outlives an attachment, so it must not be bundled into the
   107	  reader or writer protocol.
   108	
   109	Deliverable: a contract-conformance test suite, **surface-aware by
   110	construction**. The harness takes a transport's factories plus its declared
   111	surface set and runs only the applicable blocks: shared laws (result
   112	shapes, envelope validation, descriptor identity, close scope) always;
   113	reader laws (cursor provenance — mint / successor / gap-recovery only —
   114	non-destructive multi-reader reads, anchors) only for handles declaring a
   115	reader surface; writer laws only for writers. A reader suite cannot run
   116	against a WebSocket handle, and a harness that assumes it can will be
   117	discovered in Phase 4 rather than here.
   118	
   119	The suite is *authored* in Phase 1 and first *demonstrated* in Phase 2.
   120	Phase 1 has no transport, so it proves nothing on its own; that is expected
   121	and is not a reason to defer writing it.
   122	
   123	## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)
   124	
   125	The reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:
   126	
   127	- Logical-stream identity minted at `create!`, per *Settled before Phase 1*.
   128	- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns
   129	  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail
   130	  recovery cursor. Eviction applies whatever any reader is doing — a
   131	  retention policy that waits for readers is forbidden, not deferred (see
   132	  *Boundary of this plan*).
   133	- A declared **high-water** figure alongside capacity and eviction mode: the
   134	  occupancy at which a reader that is watching wants to know. The ring buffer
   135	  does nothing with it — it holds no reader positions, so it cannot know
   136	  anyone's backlog. It is carried in the declaration so a flow-control
   137	  interpreter has one place to read it from (4d).
   138	- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;
   139	  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry the logical-stream identity
   140	  (`cursor-mismatch` / `invalid-cursor` detectable); valid across handles
   141	  of the same logical stream.
   142	- One coherent state per operation (single atom; one deref per `next`),
   143	  satisfying the Concurrency section.
   144	
   145	Explicitly absent: waiters, drain, take, seq views, `closed?`, and **live
   146	resize** — resize is compatible with the contract but proves nothing the
   147	slice needs, and fixed declared capacity is sufficient for deposit
   148	admission. It follows the slice.
   149	
   150	The Phase 1 conformance suite runs here for the first time, with the full
   151	surface set. Ring-buffer-specific tests cover retention, anchors, and
   152	eviction reporting.
   153	
   154	## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip
   155	
   156	The portable identity story, still in-memory, and constructible without
   157	WebSockets: the contract explicitly permits a host composition to keep a
   158	directory, which is what makes a ring buffer's descriptor resolvable on its
   159	own host.
   160	
   161	- `descriptor` on a ring buffer handle yields a plain-data envelope that
   162	  survives the transit codec structurally unchanged.
   163	- A **host-kept local directory** — a map from stream identity to live
   164	  handle, owned by the test composition, not by DaoStream and not by any
   165	  transport. It is deliberately test-only: the ws serving side in Phase 4
   166	  resolves `:ws/path` against its own served-stream table and does not
   167	  reuse this. Say so in the code, so nobody later mistakes it for
   168	  infrastructure.
   169	- Round-trip test: descriptor → encode → decode → `attach!` against that
   170	  composition → handle on the same logical stream; a kept cursor resumes
   171	  through the new handle. This is where the kept-cursor promise is proven,
   172	  on a transport that has a reader surface.
   173	- `not-found` for descriptors nothing backs.
   174	
   175	**Decision gate — descriptor key set.** The contract leaves the descriptor
   176	envelope TBD and this phase produces the evidence for settling it. Phase 4
   177	must not start against provisional keys: review the round trip's key set,
   178	settle it in `dao.stream.md`, and settle `:ws/…` in `dao.stream.ws.md`
   179	against it. Provisional keys carried into a wire format under delivery
   180	pressure become permanent by accident.
   181	
   182	## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition
   183	
   184	Per `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —
   185	transport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:
   186	
   187	### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)
   188	
   189	- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting (`ok` or
   190	  `invalid-descriptor` only — see *Settled before Phase 1*); the handle is
   191	  writer+closable only; `append!` with transient `full` (including while
   192	  the connection is still establishing), `invalid-value` for unencodable
   193	  values, `closed` once down; `close!` means disconnect, reattachable;
   194	  connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited.
   195	- **Server side**: the accepted-connection handle — writer+closable, minted
   196	  from a socket the runtime handed over, so neither `create!` nor `attach!`
   197	  produces it — with the same `full`, `closed`, `transport-error`,
   198	  send-racing-close, and identity rules as the client handle; plus that
   199	  connection's own boundary adapter for its inbound events.
   200	- **Transport constructor**: the entry functions a host puts in its dispatch
   201	  table are closures this constructor produces, having received the deposit
   202	  destination and its admission declaration. Bare namespace vars could reach
   203	  that state only through namespace globals.
   204	- **Handshake**: encode and recognize the served-stream presentation and the
   205	  authoritative disclaimer specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` — wire protocol, so
   206	  both ends of it are transport, not composition.
   207	- **Boundary adapter**: deposits every inbound event — payload and
   208	  lifecycle, including how the connection resolved, judging nothing — into the
   209	  host-composed deposit destination — one medium per boundary in this slice,
   210	  the granularity being composition policy per `dao.stream.ws.md` — as
   211	  `:dao.stream/attachment`-tagged envelopes, enforcing admission at assembly
   212	  against the declaration passed alongside the destination, and tearing the
   213	  connection down on any non-`ok` deposit result.
   214	- No callbacks in the public surface; the legacy `on-open!`/`on-message!`/
   215	  `on-close!` shape does not reappear.
   216	- **Dependency check**: `v2/ws.cljc` requires only `dao.stream` — never
   217	  the ringbuffer namespace. The ws transport depends on the protocols; the
   218	  ring buffer is the slice's composition partner (deposit destination,
   219	  served stream), wired by the host composition, not by the transport. A
   220	  transport requiring another transport is the legacy registry defect
   221	  reborn.
   222	
   223	### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)
   224	
   225	Serving is what makes a stream remotely attachable, and nothing in the
   226	transport transfers a served stream's values to a connected socket. Under
   227	the contract that transfer is an interpreter: it reads one stream through
   228	its own cursor and appends to another. Streams notify no one, so nothing
   229	drives it — it polls, and when `next` answers `blocked` it schedules itself
   230	and asks again.
   231	
   232	That loop is not WebSocket-specific. It reads *any* reader handle and
   233	appends to *any* writer handle: ring buffer to socket, ring buffer to ring
   234	buffer, the code is identical. Its transport-agnosticism is the proof that
   235	it belongs neither inside a transport nor inside one composition. It is a
   236	third layer between them.
   237	
   238	`forward!` owns the three disciplines that are the same everywhere and are
   239	the easiest things in this slice to get wrong:
   240	
   241	- `full` **does not advance the cursor** — nothing was appended, and
   242	  treating the value as forwarded drops it silently;
   243	- `blocked` yields to the scheduler rather than spinning — on a cooperative
   244	  event loop a spin starves the runtime, so the send buffer never drains
   245	  and `full` never clears;
   246	- `end` closes the destination.
   247	
   248	It owns nothing else. The rest are arguments, because each has different
   249	right answers in different compositions:
   250	
   251	- **the scheduler** — "run this again later": `setTimeout` on Node, a
   252	  sleeping thread on the JVM. Cadence is the concern of the runtime driving
   253	  the interpreter, never of the stream (contract, *The readiness
   254	  extension*);
   255	- **the `gap` policy** — resume at the recovery cursor, or tear down. A
   256	  serving pump may skip evicted values; an interpreter that dropped
   257	  submitted work must not. Same outcome, opposite correct responses;
   258	- **the batch budget** — how many values to drain before yielding, which
   259	  exists only because a cooperative event loop needs yielding;
   260	- **starting it.** It never self-installs. The host wires it and starts it
   261	  explicitly, or implicit control flow is back.
   262	- **stopping and restarting it.** A stopped forwarder makes no calls at all —
   263	  it does not poll — and **keeps its cursor**. Restarting resumes from exactly
   264	  that position, which is what makes flow control (4d) need no protocol for
   265	  saying where to continue: the sender never lost its place, so a paused
   266	  connection resumes without being told anything.
   267	
   268	(If `forward!` is respecified as a single step returning its next state rather
   269	than a loop that reschedules itself — see the open items — stop and start
   270	become properties of the driver instead of operations on the loop, and this
   271	bullet collapses into "the driver stops calling it.")
   272	
   273	`forward!` is written against the contract rather than against a named
   274	transport, so it is the one interpreter that must be **total over the outcome
   275	algebra** — every outcome of `next` and of `append!`, including those its
   276	particular endpoints never produce. Beyond the disciplines above that means an
   277	explicit branch for destination `closed`, `invalid-value`, and
   278	`transport-error`, and for source `cursor-mismatch` and `invalid-cursor` (its
   279	start cursor is caller-supplied and may not belong to the source). Terminate
   280	and report rather than retry: only `full` and `blocked` are the retryable
   281	answers. Otherwise the contract's totality is bought by moving partiality into
   282	its first consumer.
   283	
   284	Testable with two ring buffers and no socket, so it may be written any time
   285	after Phase 2; only its *use* is Phase 4.
   286	
   287	### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)
   288	
   289	What is left once the loop is infrastructure is the wiring and policy only
   290	the host has. This is a named deliverable, not test wiring, and it must
   291	define:
   292	
   293	- the serving lifecycle: what starts and stops an endpoint, and who owns it;
   294	- the `:ws/path` → served-stream resolution table, host-owned data;
   295	- which identities this endpoint serves — the resolution table's contents.
   296	  The handshake's wire vocabulary is not composition; it belongs to 4a;
   297	- how a connection is associated with the served stream it joined, and how
   298	  that identity reaches deposited events;
   299	- one `forward!` per connection, from the served stream to that
   300	  connection's ws handle, supplied with the scheduler, cadence, batch
   301	  budget, and `gap` policy this composition chooses;
   302	- whether inbound payload events are interpreted into appends on the served
   303	  stream, and if so by which interpreter — the boundary itself judges
   304	  nothing;
   305	- close behavior: what a server-side owner closing the logical stream does
   306	  to connected attachments.
   307	
   308	### 4d — Flow control
   309	
   310	Without this, a reader that falls behind loses data: the deposit medium evicts
   311	its oldest values and the reader is told it missed them. That is honest, and
   312	right for a live feed where the newest value is the one that matters. It is
   313	wrong as the only option, and it makes eviction the normal mode rather than
   314	the backstop it should be.
   315	
   316	Nothing here is a transport change. Pause requests travel as ordinary values
   317	on the socket the client already writes to, and the serving side reads them
   318	off its own request medium (see `dao.stream.ws.md`, *Serving*). Two
   319	interpreters, one per end.
   320	
   321	- **The reader publishes its own backlog.** Only it can: a stream holds no
   322	  reader positions, so neither the medium nor the adapter can know how far
   323	  behind anyone is. Occupancy relative to *your* cursor is private to you.
   324	  When it passes the declared high-water figure, the reader asks for a pause.
   325	- **A pause is a lease, not a switch.** The request carries a duration — hold
   326	  for *n* — and the reader renews while it is still behind. A switch has a
   327	  stuck state: a lost resume, or a reader that dies mid-pause, leaves the
   328	  sender stopped forever. A lease expires, so absent renewal the system
   329	  returns to sending on its own, and a dead reader is indistinguishable from
   330	  one that caught up.
   331	- **The serving composition acts on it**: stop the connection's `forward!`,
   332	  restart it when the lease lapses or the reader stops renewing. The cursor
   333	  never moved, so nothing says where to resume.
   334	- **Liveness**: the serving side pings on an interval and drops a connection
   335	  after two unanswered. A connection can die without either side being told,
   336	  and a paused forwarder would otherwise sit indefinitely on a peer that is
   337	  gone.
   338	- **Caps**: a maximum lease duration, so one request cannot buy an hour, and a
   339	  maximum consecutive paused time, after which the connection is dropped
   340	  rather than renewed forever.
   341	
   342	**Why this cannot be turned against the server.** The served stream evicts on
   343	its own schedule regardless of who is attached, so a client that pauses and
   344	never returns causes the server to retain nothing extra — it simply falls
   345	behind and comes back to a `gap`. The loss moves upstream, bounded by the
   346	served stream's retention, and lands where the producer can be told about it.
   347	A client that pauses forever harms only itself. The caps above make that
   348	bounded rather than merely self-correcting.
   349	
   350	## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end
   351	
   352	Two processes, not two compositions in one. A same-process socket test is
   353	worth writing earlier for diagnostics, but it cannot show that a descriptor
   354	is self-contained or that no accidental shared state carries identity
   355	across — and those are central contract promises.
   356	
   357	Process A creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B
   358	receives the descriptor as data, attaches, and appends outbound; B's host
   359	boundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in B
   360	reads that medium with cursors. State the bootstrap channel explicitly:
   361	how B obtains A's descriptor is part of the test, not an assumption.
   362	
   363	Kill the connection, then verify four separable facts:
   364	
   365	1. Detachment is observable on both sides — B's handle answers `closed`,
   366	   A's boundary deposits the departure.
   367	2. The served stream survives on A, and `attach!` with the same descriptor
   368	   rejoins the same logical stream.
   369	3. Two simultaneous attachments to the same stream carry distinct
   370	   `:dao.stream/attachment` values, and every event class deposited for one —
   371	   payload, opened, closed, error, resolution — carries its own.
   372	4. B's cursor **on B's deposit medium** still resumes — that medium
   373	   outlives the socket. The cursor is not handed to `attach!` and not used
   374	   through the ws handle, which has no reader surface. WebSocket-level
   375	   resumption — what history a rejoining client receives — is deferred in
   376	   `dao.stream.ws.md` and is not tested here.
   377	
   378	## Host matrix
   379	
   380	- **Phases 1–3** are pure `.cljc` with no transport: they must compile and
   381	  pass on clj, cljs (Node), and cljd. Nothing in them is host-specific, and
   382	  a parity break here is cheapest to find here.
   383	- **Phase 4–5** target clj and cljs (Node) — Node server plus Node client is
   384	  the slice. Browser-client behavior is not validated by a Node target and
   385	  is out of the slice. cljd ws follows once the slice is proven; beware the
   386	  `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap.
   387	- **`forward.cljc` is host-agnostic** — its only host dependency is the
   388	  scheduler handed to it — so it compiles and its ring-buffer-to-ring-buffer
   389	  tests pass on all three hosts, including where the ws transport does not
   390	  yet exist.
   391	- Per host, name explicitly: how transient send-buffer `full` is detected,
   392	  how a send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec
   393	  produces structurally identical values across all three.
   394	
   395	## What v1 callers lose, and what replaces it
   396	
   397	The contract is a clean-room redesign: `dao.stream.md` neither mentions v1 nor
   398	owes it compatibility, and nothing in `dao.stream` may consult this table.
   399	It lives here because this plan is the migration, and because the 52 `open!`
   400	call sites need to know what happened to the call they were making. It is
   401	consumed with the rest of this document.
   402	
   403	| v1 (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)     | v2                            | Why                                                                                       |
   404	|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   405	| `IDaoStreamReader` — `next`         | reader — `cursor`, `next`     | Cursors are minted by the stream instead of built inline as `{:position 0}`, so a transport owns its cursor representation and `cursor-mismatch` becomes detectable. |
   406	| `IDaoStreamWriter` — `append!`      | writer — `append!`            | Unchanged in shape; the outcome set is now exhaustive and `append!` never throws on a closed stream — it answers `closed`. |
   407	| `IDaoStreamBound` — `close!`        | closable — `close!`           | Outcome set is `{ok}`, idempotent.                                                        |
   408	| `IDaoStreamBound` — `closed?`       | **gone**                      | A predicate answer is stale the moment it returns; operate the handle and read the outcome. |
   409	| `IDaoStreamWaitable` — 2 fns        | **gone**                      | No readiness extension. A `blocked` reader retries when it chooses; cadence belongs to the runtime driving it. |
   410	| `IDaoStreamDrainable` / `drain-one!` | **gone**                     | Destructive read makes one reader's progress every other reader's data loss.               |
   411	| `open!` multimethod, `defopen` macro | **gone**                     | Ambient namespace-global registry with load-time registration. Replaced by an ordinary host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to `{create attach}`, looked up by the host. |
   412	| `open!` on a descriptor              | `create!` or `attach!`        | v1 conflated them. Creation takes a creation specification; attachment takes a portable descriptor and never creates. |
   413	| `take!!`                             | **gone**                      | Blocking. JVM-only by construction, and unimplementable on cljs/cljd.                      |
   414	| `strict-vec`                         | **gone**                      | Throws on ordinary operational signals; those are outcomes.                                |
   415	| `->seq`                              | **gone**                      | An interpreter over a stream, not part of the contract (see the contract's Composition).   |
   416	| bare `:ok` / `:blocked` / `:end` / `:daostream/gap` returns | outcome maps under `:dao.stream/…` | Every operation returns an open map keyed by `:dao.stream/outcome`, so extensions add keys without breaking consumers. |
   417	| `{:position 0}` cursors              | transport-owned cursor values | There is no `seek`; every valid cursor comes from the stream.                              |
   418	| `descriptor` via handle metadata     | `descriptor` operation        | Identity is universal, answered by every handle, and outlives any attachment.              |
   419	
   420	Migrating a call site is therefore mostly mechanical — `open!` splits, results
   421	become maps, cursors come from `cursor` — with two places that need a decision
   422	rather than a translation: anything relying on `closed?` must be restructured
   423	to act on an outcome, and anything relying on `drain-one!` or `take!!` needs a
   424	cursor and a retry policy its runtime owns.
   425	
   426	## Boundary of this plan
   427	
   428	Forbidden, not deferred:
   429	
   430	- **Retention that waits for readers.** A stream that declines to evict until
   431	  every attached cursor has passed a position is one line from the design
   432	  above and has a failure mode none of the rest do: a single stalled or
   433	  departed reader holds the stream open indefinitely, and a hostile one does
   434	  it on purpose. Bounded retention must stay bounded. Loss is reported as
   435	  `gap`; it is never prevented by waiting.
   436	
   437	Not in this plan, by design:
   438	
   439	- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)
   440	  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.
   441	- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).
   442	- The waiter/readiness extension, Shibi, ws resumption protocol, cursor
   443	  serialization across hosts, live ring buffer resize — deferred in the
   444	  contract, deferred here.
   445	
   446	## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork
   447	
   448	The v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,
   449	not to live forever. The slice is complete when Phases 1–5 pass on clj and
   450	cljs; it is explicitly **incomplete on cljd** until the cljd ws transport
   451	lands, and the end condition is not met before then.
   452	
   453	When the slice is complete and the last consumer has migrated (under its
   454	own plan), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a single decision is taken
   455	explicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to `dao.stream`, or keeps its name
   456	permanently. An undecided coexistence of both namespaces is a defect of the
   457	migration, not a steady state.

