## 2. My withdrawals and downgrades

- **A1 (`attach! ok`) — downgrade High → Medium documentation defect.** I withdraw the claim that `pending` is required. The contract explicitly permits remotely knowable establishment answers to arrive later ([dao.stream.md:252](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:252), [dao.stream.md:277](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:277)); the real defect is that `ok` is worded as though an attachment to an existing stream already exists.
- **A6 (IO model denies mutation) — downgrade High → Medium wording defect.** In context, “never a request for something to happen and be reported back” is aimed at future asynchronous completion, not at denying immediate state transitions ([dao.stream.md:129](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:129)). It still needs the linearization wording I proposed.
- **A7 (adapter teardown) — retain Medium but withdraw my status-stream remedy.** A mandatory “always-admitting” status stream merely recurses when that deposit fails. The operational design is defensible; the missing correction is a narrowly documented terminal-boundary rule.
- **A9 (conformance harness) — narrow, not withdraw.** The absence of a declaration schema and unconditional “close scope” remain defects ([implementation-plan.md:109](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:109)); I withdraw any implication that every shared law necessarily requires every transport entry function.
- **A8 (`close!`) — retain Medium as a required semantic decision, not a mandatory new outcome.** Adding `transport-error` is one solution; defining close as an irrevocable logical transition with best-effort cleanup is another.

I am dropping none outright; A1’s demanded `pending` outcome and A7’s proposed status-stream mechanism are the substantive remedies I withdraw.

## 3. The three split issues

### `attach!`: `ok` versus `pending`

A was not right at High severity. A WebSocket `ok` can honestly mean: “a locally valid attachment handle targeting this descriptor identity now exists, and establishment has begun.” It does **not** honestly mean: “the far end has confirmed that the named stream exists.”

The contract tries to say both at once: the row claims a handle “on an attachment to the logical stream” and then qualifies that remote establishment arrives later ([dao.stream.md:277](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:277)). The WebSocket spec is clearer that the attachment is still being established ([dao.stream.ws.md:293](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:293)).

The correction is therefore wording, not `pending`:

> `ok` — a local handle targeting the logical-stream identity in the descriptor exists, and any locally initiable attachment work was accepted. It makes no claim that a remote endpoint has confirmed the stream’s existence; that resolution arrives on the transport-declared channel.

`pending` would also be coherent, but it adds an outcome without resolving what the returned handle represents and needlessly distinguishes local and remote transports at the contract level.

### Flow-control measurement

Yes: 4d is unimplementable as written. Opaque cursors cannot yield distance from retained tail, and the reader cannot infer that distance from the existing outcome algebra ([dao.stream.md:381](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:381), [implementation-plan.md:321](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.implementation-plan.md:321)).

For the semantics presently written—retained occupancy relative to a cursor—the clean fix is a standardized optional `:dao.stream/lag` observation on `next` results, defined as a momentary count relative to the returned/current cursor. A transport-owned `lag(handle, cursor)` is acceptable for this slice but makes 4d ring-buffer-specific; a reader-owned work queue measures a different quantity, namely fetched-but-unprocessed work. Move high-water configuration to the reader composition.

If the project does not want to standardize lag semantics now, defer 4d. It must not be implemented by inspecting cursor internals.

### Adapter teardown

Closing the socket the boundary itself holds is not interpretation of the inbound event. It is the boundary ceasing to claim that it can turn host events into stream data after its sole emission path has failed.

Nevertheless, the governing prose literally says the adapter “invokes nothing” and is only a map of transforms plus append ([datom.world.md:65](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:65), [datom.world.md:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:70)); teardown is an additional host action. C has the right resolution: retain the design, document one narrow terminal exception—after a non-`ok` deposit, the boundary may close only the host resource it owns, may invoke nothing above itself, and may perform no semantic recovery or retry.

